WEBVTT

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:10.639
<v><b>Interviewer:</b></v> What do you think are the benefits of living in a multicultural society?

00:00:12.319 --> 00:00:16.884
<v><b>Ida:</b></v> I think the benefits of living in a multicultural society are all positive.

00:00:17.577 --> 00:00:19.325
I don’t think there’s a negative you can take from it.

00:00:19.565 --> 00:00:24.813
You experience different aspects of different cultures, food, music, perspectives,

00:00:24.906 --> 00:00:29.720
and I think, most importantly, it allows you to gain a perspective on your own existence,

00:00:29.773 --> 00:00:34.325
almost sort of, that the only way of life is not yours, there are different ways of looking at it.

00:00:34.938 --> 00:00:38.828
And for me, personally, I, I grew up, sort of, all over the world,

00:00:38.908 --> 00:00:41.388
we moved to a different country every three years of my life.

00:00:42.408 --> 00:00:46.309
And, when there, we were always part of a very international community,

00:00:46.334 --> 00:00:50.328
but also experiencing different countries in a very multicultural way and, a,

00:00:50.353 --> 00:00:55.923
a kind of, seeing multiculturalism in different countries and how it works in different countries…

00:00:55.948 --> 00:00:56.939
<v><b>Tim:</b></v> Which countries were they?

00:00:57.072 --> 00:01:02.704
<v><b>Ida:</b></v> Um, to – from Uruguay to Italy to Austria to Belgium…places like that.

00:01:03.311 --> 00:01:08.786
And so, I think it just, it allows you to be part of a different community

00:01:08.806 --> 00:01:13.049
and allows you to be more open-minded and more understanding and…

00:01:13.130 --> 00:01:15.254
<v><b>Tim:</b></v> Yeah. That’s so true, yeah.

00:01:16.273 --> 00:01:17.126
<v><b>Ben:</b></v> Yeah, yeah, no, I agree.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:22.470
I mean, for me, I’ve got, I’ve got a young son who’s about to start school,

00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:26.240
er, later this year and so we’ve had to try and choose a school for him.

00:01:26.331 --> 00:01:32.422
And one of the things that was important to me was to choose a school

00:01:32.442 --> 00:01:36.063
that did have a lot of different cultures, a lot of different languages and,

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:41.531
um, I didn’t realize that it was important until we actually started looking at different schools.

00:01:41.558 --> 00:01:42.185
<v><b>Tim:</b></v> Yeah, yeah.

00:01:42.398 --> 00:01:44.931
<v><b>Ben:</b></v> And then there was one school in particular that really stood out

00:01:44.958 --> 00:01:49.832
and one of the reasons was because, not only do they have lots of different languages,

00:01:49.858 --> 00:01:53.620
cultures, backgrounds, but they really, they really celebrate that,

00:01:54.040 --> 00:02:01.249
and so they really encourage the kids and the parents to contribute to the school and to,

00:02:01.716 --> 00:02:07.663
to bring part of themselves and their culture and their identity to the school and really celebrate that.

00:02:07.743 --> 00:02:09.392
I, I thought that was wonderful.

00:02:09.605 --> 00:02:13.223
<v><b>Ida:</b></v> That reminds me of an experience I had in school in first grade when I was living in Uruguay,

00:02:13.436 --> 00:02:16.937
we had this presentation and we all had to sing a song that they’d written,

00:02:17.378 --> 00:02:19.446
and we all had to wear our national dress.

00:02:19.519 --> 00:02:22.987
And the song was like ‘Ida is from Sweden, Fabiola’s from Brazil…’

00:02:23.181 --> 00:02:26.940
and so everybody – it was a class of 20 kids and we were all from different places

00:02:26.973 --> 00:02:29.541
and it’s just really important, and I think it just

00:02:30.188 --> 00:02:32.922
– I then become very aware, when I am in a place that is…

00:02:33.429 --> 00:02:38.206
not that, and it makes me slightly uncomfortable, sort of, I don’t know…

00:02:38.299 --> 00:02:42.429
<v><b>Tim:</b></v> I think it’s really important, um, especially in the times we live in now, um,

00:02:42.749 --> 00:02:48.163
and I, I think it’s, sort of, almost a, a, a natural human tendency to be slightly suspicious

00:02:48.189 --> 00:02:53.128
or afraid of, you know, anything that looks different or, sort of, ‘the other’.

00:02:53.328 --> 00:02:57.458
And I think multicultural societies encourage you to understand that,

00:02:57.505 --> 00:03:04.432
um, different people act, talk, dress, er, behave in different ways, and that, um,

00:03:04.825 --> 00:03:06.368
and that, you know, variety is good.

00:03:07.648 --> 00:03:08.610
<v><b>Ida:</b></v> Especially at the moment, I think,

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:13.691
when we’re living in a time when ‘othering’ is coming back in a big way.

00:03:13.737 --> 00:03:17.178
<v><b>Tim:</b></v> Yes, and suspicion is kind of like, you know, part of, part of the landscape again.

00:03:17.252 --> 00:03:20.724
I think it’s really important to understand other people

00:03:20.758 --> 00:03:22.907
and other cultures and to be empathetic towards that.

00:03:22.913 --> 00:03:23.773
<v><b>Ida:</b></v> Absolutely.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:25.040
<v><b>Ben:</b></v> Yeah, yeah.